Give the extension host Worker its own memory budget instead of inheriting the main thread's --max-old-space-size.

NodeRuntimeController.swift starts the main process with
--max-old-space-size=256 (a deliberate, mobile-conscious budget for the
main thread's own footprint). extensionHostConnection.ts's iOS
worker_threads branch (ios-exthost-no-fork.diff) builds its Worker's own
execArgv/resourceLimits from `opts.execArgv`, which is just
`process.execArgv` (the main process's own flags) minus --inspect --
meaning that same 256MB ceiling was being silently re-applied to the
extension host's own, separate V8 isolate, not just stripped like the
jitless/wasm flags already are.

Confirmed as a real, deterministic bug via a captured node-stdio.log, not
guessed: at 256MB, the extension host Worker OOMs and gets torn down and
restarted roughly 15-20 seconds after every single boot --

  Error [ERR_WORKER_OUT_OF_MEMORY]: Worker terminated due to reaching
  memory limit: JS heap out of memory

-- reproducing identically in both the pre- and post-UI-test app launches
of the same simulator-test.yml run (run 35969346618). 256MB is far too
little for vscode's extension host, which ordinarily runs unconstrained
(a real forked OS process inheriting the platform's own multi-GB V8
default) on desktop. This is not a real device memory-pressure issue
either -- it reproduces in the Simulator, which has ample host RAM
available -- purely a self-imposed V8 old-space ceiling, unrelated to
iOS's actual jetsam memory limit (a separate, much higher ceiling this
change doesn't touch). A reconnect-and-relaunch cycle already built into
vscode's own client/server protocol silently recovers (a second Worker
takes over), which is why this had gone unnoticed: the UI still renders
correctly a few seconds later, no visible crash, just a slower, wasteful
cold start on every single boot.

This went through several rounds of "fix, re-verify against a real
simulator-test.yml log, discover the picture is more nuanced than it
looked, adjust" -- all real findings, not guesses, kept here so a future
session doesn't repeat the same dead ends:

- 512MB: re-verified via simulator-test.yml run 35972671722 -- the
  OOM+restart pattern was gone in one of the run's two app launches but
  still present in the other. Looked like non-deterministic timing.
- 1024MB: re-verified via a repeat=2 run (35975535474, 4 app launches),
  which revealed the real pattern isn't random at all: the very first,
  cold-cache launch right after a fresh `simctl install` ("pre-uitest")
  OOM'd 2/2 times, while the second, warm launch a UI test's own relaunch
  triggers moments later on the same already-installed app ("post-uitest")
  never did, 2/2 times. So 1024MB *did* fully and reliably fix the warm
  case (every app open after the very first).
- 2048MB: re-verified via another repeat=2 run (35979036132) -- changed
  NOTHING about the cold-launch case. It still OOMs 2/2 times, same
  connection/exit pattern, at essentially the same wall-clock offset from
  launch (~12-20s) as it did all the way back at 256MB. A crash genuinely
  gated by hitting an old-space ceiling should take proportionally longer
  to reproduce as that ceiling is raised 8x (256->2048); seeing zero
  timing change across that whole range means the cold-launch crash is
  very likely *not* actually gated by maxOldGenerationSizeMb the way the
  warm-launch case clearly was. Left at 2048 anyway (a real, confirmed
  improvement for the warm/common case, no observed downside), but raising
  it further is unlikely to close the remaining cold-launch gap and
  shouldn't be tried again without new evidence of what's actually
  consuming memory during that specific one-time path. Leading suspect,
  not yet confirmed: first-run default-profile extension installation
  (the "Started initializing default profile extensions..." log line only
  ever appears on a fresh install) -- see README for the open item.

Net effect: the extension host now reliably starts clean on every launch
except the very first one after a fresh install, which still OOMs once
and self-heals via the existing reconnect-and-relaunch cycle -- same
symptom as before this patch (a few extra seconds of cold start, no
visible crash), just now confined to first-run only instead of every
single boot.

